                <pb facs="0519" n="485" sameAs="frus:frus1897/pg485"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Taylor to Mr.
                            Sherman.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1897-04-23"><hi rendition="#italic">April 23, 1897</hi></date>.
                        (Received Mar. 7.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 686.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to Department’s telegrams of
                    January 22 and March 1 and to my Nos. 636, of January-29, and 655, of March 3,
                    of the present year, relative to certain American citizens arrested in Cuba, I
                    have the honor to inform you that I have received a note from the minister for
                    foreign affairs, informing me officially that the American citizen Jose Gonzales
                    Curbelo, one of the prisoners named in the first-mentioned telegram, was placed
                    at liberty on the 30th of last March.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hannis Taylor.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
